# DEPLOY TARGETS

One lab, every target. `docker-compose.yml` is the single definition of the lab. The
other targets run that same compose file on a **lab host**: a Debian 12 VM configured by
`deploy/ansible/site.yml`.

| Target | Player picks | Built by | Lives in |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Docker | This machine | `docker compose` | `docker-compose.yml` |
| Local VM | This machine (VM) | Vagrant | `deploy/vagrant/Vagrantfile` |
| Home lab, ESXi | Their ESXi host | Vagrant + `vagrant-vmware-esxi` | `deploy/vagrant/Vagrantfile` |
| Home lab, Proxmox | Their Proxmox host | Terraform `bpg/proxmox` | `deploy/terraform/proxmox/` |
| Cloud | AWS | Terraform `hashicorp/aws` | `deploy/terraform/aws/` |

## Rules

- `deploy/` is template code: copy it unchanged into every lab. Lab-specific work stays in
  `docker-compose.yml`, `build/`, `evidence/`.
- Ansible always runs **inside** the lab host against localhost (Vagrant `ansible_local`,
  cloud-init on Terraform targets). Never require Ansible on the player's machine
  (it doesn't run on Windows).
- Terraform targets fetch the lab from GitHub at the pinned commit
  (`codeload.github.com/<repo>/tar.gz/<sha>`): the lab repository must be public.
- No host port bindings in compose: services are reached from the attack box on the
  lab network, on every target (the attack box runs next to the lab, on the lab host).
- Secrets: the launch token reaches the VM only as the compose `.env` (0600) or the
  cloud-init file `/etc/cyberctf/lab.env` (0600). Never commit `.env`, `*.tfvars`, state.
- A lab that needs several machines (pfSense, AD) adds them to the Vagrantfile and
  Terraform modules and drops targets it can't support from `providers` in metadata.
